WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.702
- I think we, as I was
saying a few minutes ago,

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:04.504
I think we have
to go on offense.

00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:09.275 align:start size:88% position:13%
So often, Republicans
are so afraid to lean in

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:11.478
and take the offensive position.

00:00:11.478 --> 00:00:14.814
And we sort of apologize many
times for being conservative.

00:00:14.814 --> 00:00:16.516
And we shouldn't allow the left

00:00:16.516 --> 00:00:18.551 align:start size:81% position:19%
to, one, define
us or demonize us.

00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:20.854 align:start size:84% position:16%
And we have allowed
that for far too long.

00:00:20.854 --> 00:00:23.256 align:start size:88% position:13%
We have to stand up
and start pushing back.

00:00:23.256 --> 00:00:25.925
(upbeat music)

00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:31.831
- I'm Dave Rubin.

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:34.267
And joining me today
is the 47th governor

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:36.169
of the great state of Arkansas.

00:00:36.169 --> 00:00:37.570
Governor Sarah Huckabee Sanders,

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.205
welcome to "The Rubin Report."

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:40.640 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Thank you so
much for having me.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.742 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's great to finally
get to connect

00:00:42.742 --> 00:00:44.344
and be part of your show.

00:00:44.344 --> 00:00:45.879
- Yeah, thanks, governor.

00:00:45.879 --> 00:00:47.614
You know, we've bumped into
each other a couple times,

00:00:47.614 --> 00:00:49.649
since you've been governor,
before you were governor,

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:51.951 align:start size:97% position:3%
when you were press secretary,
I think even before that,

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:54.287
but finally making this happen.

00:00:54.287 --> 00:00:55.955
So we've got about a half hour,

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:57.657 align:start size:84% position:16%
I kind of wanna talk
about everything.

00:00:57.657 --> 00:01:01.194
Can you give everybody just,
like, maybe the minute bio

00:01:01.194 --> 00:01:04.330
before all of politics and
then we'll go from there.

00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:07.634 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Well, I've been in
politics for a long time.

00:01:07.634 --> 00:01:11.104 align:start size:84% position:16%
So my bio before that
isn't super long,

00:01:11.104 --> 00:01:15.742
but I've spent a lot more
time behind the scenes

00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:19.045
than I ever did in
front of the camera.

00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:23.450
And so, I was a political
strategist and operative,

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.752
ran campaigns all
over the country,

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:28.521 align:start size:94% position:6%
sort of accidentally
became the press secretary

00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:29.456
for Donald Trump.

00:01:29.456 --> 00:01:31.825
Amazing job, great opportunity.

00:01:31.825 --> 00:01:34.627
Loved my two and a
half years there.

00:01:34.627 --> 00:01:37.630 align:start size:94% position:6%
Grew up with a dad who was
obviously very involved

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:39.032
in politics,

00:01:39.032 --> 00:01:41.167 align:start position:0%
still one of the longest
serving governors of the state.

00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:44.537
And, you know, loved
being able to watch

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:46.139
what he was able to accomplish,

00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:48.141
the way he was able to
impact direct change

00:01:48.141 --> 00:01:49.642
on people's lives.

00:01:49.642 --> 00:01:52.078
Didn't necessarily see
myself running for office

00:01:52.078 --> 00:01:54.747
until not too long ago,

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:57.550 align:start size:94% position:6%
but really wanted to
continue in public service

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.119
after my time at
the White House.

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:02.388
Moved back home to Arkansas
and hit the ground running,

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:06.426
ran for governor, and spent two
years on the campaign trail,

00:02:06.426 --> 00:02:08.995 align:start size:94% position:6%
won in November, and we
haven't slowed down since.

00:02:08.995 --> 00:02:13.299
And it's been probably the
most amazing, and difficult,

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:17.070 align:start size:97% position:3%
but also rewarding job I
think I could possibly have.

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.873
- Yeah, and you guys are
getting a ton of stuff done

00:02:19.873 --> 00:02:22.275
that I wanna focus
on for most of this,

00:02:22.275 --> 00:02:25.345
your dad obviously
being Mike Huckabee,

00:02:25.345 --> 00:02:27.780 align:start size:97% position:3%
but let's talk about the press
secretary part under Trump

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.315
for just a couple of minutes,

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:30.717 align:start size:84% position:16%
because I think
that's where probably

00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:33.520 align:start size:84% position:16%
most people originally
know you from.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:34.721
You did that for a while.

00:02:34.721 --> 00:02:36.656 align:start size:84% position:16%
It was obviously crazy
'cause the media's

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:39.092 align:start size:94% position:6%
coming after you guys, you
know, like gangbusters.

00:02:39.092 --> 00:02:41.728 align:start size:84% position:16%
You got out before
Covid, so your timing,

00:02:41.728 --> 00:02:44.364 align:start position:0%
how do you feel about your
timing of that whole situation?

00:02:44.364 --> 00:02:46.833 align:start size:88% position:13%
- You know, I got pretty
lucky on the timing,

00:02:46.833 --> 00:02:50.270
but not to say we didn't have
our fair share of controversy

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:52.772
during my two and a half years

00:02:52.772 --> 00:02:54.607
when I was the press secretary.

00:02:54.607 --> 00:02:59.579
But it's probably the most
difficult, high stakes jobs

00:03:00.313 --> 00:03:01.814
in all of government,

00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:04.450
but it's also one of the
most amazing opportunity.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.486
I was honored that the president

00:03:06.486 --> 00:03:08.121 align:start size:84% position:16%
was willing to take
a chance on me.

00:03:08.121 --> 00:03:10.523
And even on the worst days,

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:12.692
I really loved every
minute of the job.

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:14.561 align:start size:94% position:6%
I got to work with some
of the most amazing people

00:03:14.561 --> 00:03:16.062
I've ever encountered,

00:03:16.062 --> 00:03:18.965
and I'm thankful for the
time I got to spend there.

00:03:18.965 --> 00:03:21.267
- When you watch, if you watch

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:23.570
any of these press
conferences now,

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:26.839
first with Psaki and now
it's Jean Karine Pierre,

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:28.808
and the difference
in tenor and tone

00:03:28.808 --> 00:03:30.343
of the questions they get,

00:03:30.343 --> 00:03:32.779
and, you know, my line
on Psaki was the woman

00:03:32.779 --> 00:03:34.214
simply cannot say anything true.

00:03:34.214 --> 00:03:36.482 align:start size:84% position:16%
If you asked her
her favorite number,

00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:37.684
she would say blue.

00:03:37.684 --> 00:03:39.485 align:start size:84% position:16%
And Karine
Jean-Pierre seems like

00:03:39.485 --> 00:03:41.754 align:start size:94% position:6%
an even almost exaggerated
version of that.

00:03:41.754 --> 00:03:44.123
Are you jealous in a way
or can you even believe

00:03:44.123 --> 00:03:45.525
that it's the same room

00:03:45.525 --> 00:03:48.628 align:start position:0%
that you guys were just getting
grilled on relentlessly?

00:03:48.628 --> 00:03:51.397 align:start position:0%
- You know, it's hard to believe
that it is the same place

00:03:51.397 --> 00:03:53.132
because when we would
walk into the room,

00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:55.902
you could feel, like,
the visceral anger

00:03:55.902 --> 00:03:58.304 align:start size:84% position:16%
a lot of times coming
off of the crowd.

00:03:58.304 --> 00:03:59.672
And what a lot of
people don't know

00:03:59.672 --> 00:04:01.774
is that that room is
actually really small,

00:04:01.774 --> 00:04:06.779 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they pack 100, 150
people into this tiny space

00:04:07.947 --> 00:04:10.516
just so they can yell
angry questions at you.

00:04:10.516 --> 00:04:14.554 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it is a totally completely
different atmosphere

00:04:14.554 --> 00:04:16.055
in this administration.

00:04:16.055 --> 00:04:19.425
And, you know, I wish we got
some of the easy questions

00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:20.660
that they got.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:23.563
At the same time, I sort
of feel sorry for them

00:04:23.563 --> 00:04:26.399
because we actually had
a good story to tell.

00:04:26.399 --> 00:04:29.836
We had so many successes
in the Trump Presidency,

00:04:29.836 --> 00:04:34.274
and during that time, that
I had good things to go out

00:04:34.274 --> 00:04:36.843
and sell and talk to the
American people about.

00:04:36.843 --> 00:04:39.279 align:start size:94% position:6%
They certainly don't have
that under Joe Biden.

00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:42.081
Everything they seem to
touch, they screw up.

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:45.151
And so, they have a much,
much harder job than I did

00:04:45.151 --> 00:04:48.321 align:start size:94% position:6%
because at least we had
good things to talk about.

00:04:48.321 --> 00:04:51.324
- How complex does it make it
that the federal government

00:04:51.324 --> 00:04:52.992
is screwing up so many things?

00:04:52.992 --> 00:04:54.861 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like, do you feel like
you can accomplish

00:04:54.861 --> 00:04:56.829 align:start size:97% position:3%
most of the things you
wanna accomplish as governor

00:04:56.829 --> 00:04:58.531 align:start size:84% position:16%
or obviously you can't
control everything

00:04:58.531 --> 00:05:01.901
related to the value of the
dollar and supply chain,

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:03.469
things like that?

00:05:03.469 --> 00:05:05.004 align:start size:84% position:16%
- You know, there
are certainly things

00:05:05.004 --> 00:05:08.007
that they're doing
that we can't fix

00:05:08.007 --> 00:05:09.542
and can't do a lot about,

00:05:09.542 --> 00:05:12.111 align:start size:97% position:3%
but there are so many places
where we have the ability

00:05:12.111 --> 00:05:14.347
to directly impact our states.

00:05:14.347 --> 00:05:17.684
And you're seeing a lot of
Republican governors step up

00:05:17.684 --> 00:05:20.019 align:start size:84% position:16%
and push back against
the crazy stuff

00:05:20.019 --> 00:05:21.287
coming out of Washington

00:05:21.287 --> 00:05:23.456
and the overreach of
the federal government,

00:05:23.456 --> 00:05:25.558 align:start size:94% position:6%
and frankly, take matters
into our own hands.

00:05:25.558 --> 00:05:26.993
If they're not gonna
secure the border,

00:05:26.993 --> 00:05:29.896
we're gonna crack down on drugs

00:05:29.896 --> 00:05:34.000
and things coming across
the border in our states,

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.102 align:start size:84% position:16%
making sure that we're
doing what we can

00:05:36.102 --> 00:05:38.271 align:start size:97% position:3%
where the federal government
is dropping the ball.

00:05:38.271 --> 00:05:41.641 align:start position:0%
We focused heavily on education,
criminal justice reform,

00:05:41.641 --> 00:05:43.109
tax cuts,

00:05:43.109 --> 00:05:45.745
so that we are helping
the people in our states,

00:05:45.745 --> 00:05:48.214 align:start size:97% position:3%
because we know we can't
count on anything meaningful

00:05:48.214 --> 00:05:49.816
coming out of Washington.

00:05:49.816 --> 00:05:51.050
- Yeah, you know,
I've asked this

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:53.119
to a couple of red
state governors.

00:05:53.119 --> 00:05:55.822
Like, do you feel that
there is an alliance

00:05:55.822 --> 00:05:58.858
of red states right now that
basically is coming together

00:05:58.858 --> 00:06:01.361
to solve some of
the things simply

00:06:01.361 --> 00:06:03.863 align:start size:88% position:13%
that the federal
government will not do?

00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:06.566
I saw you last time, I think
maybe two or three months ago,

00:06:06.566 --> 00:06:08.067
at a Florida blueprint event.

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:10.336
There were a bunch of other
red state governors there,

00:06:10.336 --> 00:06:12.905 align:start size:97% position:3%
and it seemed like, all
right, if Biden won't do it,

00:06:12.905 --> 00:06:14.140
we're gonna have
to do this stuff.

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:15.375
I mean, Arkansas isn't
supposed to have to deal

00:06:15.375 --> 00:06:17.076
with the border.

00:06:17.076 --> 00:06:18.511
- Absolutely.

00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:20.279 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think there's such a
greater sense of camaraderie

00:06:20.279 --> 00:06:22.749
among Republican governors.

00:06:22.749 --> 00:06:24.884 align:start size:97% position:3%
We're not in direct
competition with each other.

00:06:24.884 --> 00:06:28.221 align:start position:0%
There's a great sense of shared
practice, best practices.

00:06:28.221 --> 00:06:32.325 align:start size:94% position:6%
If I can do something here
and implement a program

00:06:32.325 --> 00:06:33.760
that really works,

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:35.595 align:start size:97% position:3%
let me share that with
other states and vice versa.

00:06:35.595 --> 00:06:38.030
There are other things
we've seen states do

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:40.800 align:start size:94% position:6%
and do really well and
wanna replicate that here.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.469 align:start size:97% position:3%
And other republican governors
have been very willing

00:06:43.469 --> 00:06:45.772
to help and work together.

00:06:45.772 --> 00:06:48.441 align:start size:84% position:16%
And when you have
that kind of coalition

00:06:48.441 --> 00:06:49.876
of conservative governors,

00:06:49.876 --> 00:06:51.511
it gives us a lot more strength

00:06:51.511 --> 00:06:54.247
to push back on the
federal government.

00:06:54.247 --> 00:06:56.582
- What should we know about
Arkansas that we don't know?

00:06:56.582 --> 00:06:58.518 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, the country doesn't
talk about Arkansas that much.

00:06:58.518 --> 00:07:00.253
We know Bill Clinton
came from Arkansas,

00:07:00.253 --> 00:07:01.721
we know your dad
came from Arkansas,

00:07:01.721 --> 00:07:03.156
we know you're down in Arkansas,

00:07:03.156 --> 00:07:06.359 align:start position:0%
but it's not talked about,
say, in the way that California

00:07:06.359 --> 00:07:08.261
or Florida might be these days.

00:07:09.429 --> 00:07:11.364
- We're glad never to
be talked about the way

00:07:11.364 --> 00:07:12.532
that California is,

00:07:12.532 --> 00:07:14.000 align:start size:88% position:13%
to be clear, Dave.
- Oh, well, yeah, yeah.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:15.935
- So we don't wanna be
on the same page as them.

00:07:15.935 --> 00:07:17.437
- Fair, fair.
- In fact,

00:07:17.437 --> 00:07:20.606
Arkansas is one of the states
that people from California

00:07:20.606 --> 00:07:21.974
are moving to.

00:07:23.676 --> 00:07:25.878
One of the largest
numbers of people

00:07:25.878 --> 00:07:27.647
that are coming into our state,

00:07:27.647 --> 00:07:31.784
I think we're the seventh
largest influx of people

00:07:31.784 --> 00:07:34.387 align:start size:84% position:16%
from California
over the last year.

00:07:34.387 --> 00:07:38.124 align:start size:97% position:3%
Nearly 10,000 people have left
there to come to Arkansas.

00:07:38.124 --> 00:07:40.293 align:start size:81% position:19%
We have an amazing
story to tell.

00:07:40.293 --> 00:07:42.628
I say all the time that
I'm the chief salesperson

00:07:42.628 --> 00:07:43.830
for Arkansas.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:45.631 align:start size:88% position:13%
We have some of the
most beautiful landscape

00:07:45.631 --> 00:07:48.935
anywhere in the country,
great quality of life.

00:07:48.935 --> 00:07:52.071 align:start size:88% position:13%
We're making significant
changes in progress

00:07:52.071 --> 00:07:55.374 align:start size:88% position:13%
when it comes to a
strong education system.

00:07:55.374 --> 00:07:56.609
We're one of the first states

00:07:56.609 --> 00:07:59.946 align:start size:88% position:13%
that will have
universal school choice

00:07:59.946 --> 00:08:01.447
over the next couple of years

00:08:01.447 --> 00:08:03.950
as we phase that in
based on legislation

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:07.320
that we passed here just in
the last couple of months.

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:09.455 align:start size:94% position:6%
We are looking at all
the things that we can do

00:08:09.455 --> 00:08:11.624
to make Arkansas the
best place to live,

00:08:11.624 --> 00:08:13.192
to work, and to raise a family.

00:08:13.192 --> 00:08:16.229 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think we've
made significant
progress on that front

00:08:16.229 --> 00:08:18.331
in just the last 100 days.

00:08:18.331 --> 00:08:19.565
I also would tell people,

00:08:19.565 --> 00:08:21.234
please don't judge
us by Bill Clinton.

00:08:21.234 --> 00:08:22.768
We have a lot better things

00:08:22.768 --> 00:08:25.037 align:start size:84% position:16%
coming out of
Arkansas than that.

00:08:25.037 --> 00:08:28.574
- How do you make sure that
that influx of new people

00:08:28.574 --> 00:08:31.544
votes the right
way and make sure

00:08:31.544 --> 00:08:33.913 align:start size:84% position:16%
that they don't import
obviously the stuff

00:08:33.913 --> 00:08:35.815
that they were fleeing in Cali.

00:08:35.815 --> 00:08:37.316
- We tell 'em all the time,

00:08:37.316 --> 00:08:40.753
every place I went when I was
campaigning across Arkansas,

00:08:40.753 --> 00:08:43.656 align:start size:84% position:16%
I went to all 75
counties in our state

00:08:43.656 --> 00:08:46.225
and held events in almost
every single place we went,

00:08:46.225 --> 00:08:48.828
we would meet people that
came from other places,

00:08:48.828 --> 00:08:51.330
largely California,
Oregon, Washington,

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:52.832
I would always remind them,

00:08:52.832 --> 00:08:55.835
don't forget why you left
those places and came here.

00:08:55.835 --> 00:09:00.006
Don't try to change us, just
enjoy the Southern hospitality.

00:09:00.006 --> 00:09:03.376 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think most of those
people were so happy

00:09:03.376 --> 00:09:06.846 align:start size:97% position:3%
to get away from the craziness
coming from the coast

00:09:06.846 --> 00:09:08.948 align:start size:84% position:16%
that they're embracing
all things Arkansas

00:09:08.948 --> 00:09:10.283
all the time here.

00:09:10.283 --> 00:09:11.751
- So you mentioned the
school choice thing.

00:09:11.751 --> 00:09:13.452 align:start size:94% position:6%
That was one of the things
I really wanna focus

00:09:13.452 --> 00:09:16.322 align:start size:88% position:13%
with you on because I
do think it's the future

00:09:16.322 --> 00:09:18.024
of education in America.

00:09:18.024 --> 00:09:21.227
You guys were one of, I think,
the first four or five states

00:09:21.227 --> 00:09:23.296
to go with school choice.

00:09:23.296 --> 00:09:24.897
What was your calculation there?

00:09:24.897 --> 00:09:27.767
I mean, were Arkansas
schools being wokeified

00:09:27.767 --> 00:09:29.969
the way that we see
so many other states?

00:09:29.969 --> 00:09:32.104 align:start size:97% position:3%
That seems odd that it would
be happening in Arkansas,

00:09:32.104 --> 00:09:35.074
but that obviously was at
least part of it, right?

00:09:35.074 --> 00:09:37.143 align:start size:84% position:16%
- There's certainly
pieces of that.

00:09:37.143 --> 00:09:40.379 align:start size:97% position:3%
And our goal is to make
sure that the crazy wokeness

00:09:40.379 --> 00:09:42.214 align:start size:81% position:19%
never sees the
light of day here.

00:09:42.214 --> 00:09:45.184
I'm not saying we don't have
instances, we certainly do,

00:09:45.184 --> 00:09:46.752 align:start size:84% position:16%
but that's not
necessarily the norm.

00:09:46.752 --> 00:09:49.055
But we wanna make sure it
doesn't become that way.

00:09:49.055 --> 00:09:52.925
We also know that
what works in Iowa,

00:09:52.925 --> 00:09:56.362 align:start size:94% position:6%
and Montana, and Texas may
not be what works here.

00:09:56.362 --> 00:09:59.465
And the same thing, just
within our small state,

00:09:59.465 --> 00:10:01.601
what works in one
corner of the state

00:10:01.601 --> 00:10:03.002
may not work in another.

00:10:03.002 --> 00:10:05.104 align:start size:97% position:3%
So we wanna provide
flexibility so that students

00:10:05.104 --> 00:10:07.139 align:start size:94% position:6%
are getting the
education that they need,

00:10:07.139 --> 00:10:09.575 align:start size:94% position:6%
and frankly, the education
that they deserve.

00:10:09.575 --> 00:10:13.479 align:start position:0%
We wanna empower parents to
make the best decisions possible

00:10:13.479 --> 00:10:16.749
about what and how their
kids can best be educated.

00:10:16.749 --> 00:10:19.552 align:start size:97% position:3%
So we're putting the power
back into the hands of parents

00:10:19.552 --> 00:10:20.786
instead of the other way.

00:10:20.786 --> 00:10:23.422
I say all the time,
it's very simple,

00:10:23.422 --> 00:10:25.124
when people ask me
what's the difference

00:10:25.124 --> 00:10:29.028
between a Democrat
and a Republican, to
me, it's very easy.

00:10:29.028 --> 00:10:31.964
If you want somebody to
make decisions for you

00:10:31.964 --> 00:10:33.733
and you want that to
be the government,

00:10:33.733 --> 00:10:35.001
then you're a Democrat.

00:10:35.001 --> 00:10:37.236
If you wanna empower individuals

00:10:37.236 --> 00:10:39.205
and you wanna make
your own decisions,

00:10:39.205 --> 00:10:40.506
then you're a Republican.

00:10:40.506 --> 00:10:42.008
And that's exactly
what we're doing.

00:10:42.008 --> 00:10:44.477 align:start size:97% position:3%
We're putting power back
into the hands of the people

00:10:44.477 --> 00:10:45.978 align:start size:81% position:19%
instead of letting
the government

00:10:45.978 --> 00:10:47.813
dictate the rules of the game.

00:10:47.813 --> 00:10:50.416
- Can you explain
how that philosophy

00:10:50.416 --> 00:10:52.485 align:start size:84% position:16%
actually is reflective
of school choice?

00:10:52.485 --> 00:10:55.121
'Cause to me, school choice
is such a winning argument

00:10:55.121 --> 00:10:57.156 align:start position:0%
for Republicans for the
reasons that you just laid out.

00:10:57.156 --> 00:10:58.124
But can you actually talk about,

00:10:58.124 --> 00:10:59.692
like, what it actually means?

00:10:59.692 --> 00:11:00.893
Okay, you have school choice,

00:11:00.893 --> 00:11:02.395 align:start size:84% position:16%
like, what does
that actually mean?

00:11:03.562 --> 00:11:04.997
- I think there's
a couple things.

00:11:04.997 --> 00:11:07.366
For us, it means
that you can choose

00:11:07.366 --> 00:11:09.735 align:start size:84% position:16%
whether it's another
public school

00:11:09.735 --> 00:11:12.238
somewhere in your area
or around this state.

00:11:12.238 --> 00:11:14.407 align:start size:94% position:6%
If it's a private school,
if it's homeschool,

00:11:14.407 --> 00:11:16.809 align:start size:97% position:3%
if it's a parochial school,
if it's charter school,

00:11:16.809 --> 00:11:20.479 align:start size:84% position:16%
you get to decide
what is the best place

00:11:20.479 --> 00:11:23.482
that my child can go
and get the education

00:11:23.482 --> 00:11:25.618
that best fits their needs.

00:11:25.618 --> 00:11:27.253
I'm a mom, I have three kids,

00:11:27.253 --> 00:11:29.088
and I can tell you
all three of my kids

00:11:29.088 --> 00:11:31.891
are completely different and
they need different things.

00:11:31.891 --> 00:11:35.961 align:start size:97% position:3%
So allowing me the opportunity
to tailor their education

00:11:35.961 --> 00:11:39.632
to fit what is best gonna put
them on the path to success

00:11:39.632 --> 00:11:41.534
is what we're trying
to implement here.

00:11:41.534 --> 00:11:44.437
So the funding that we
give for each student

00:11:44.437 --> 00:11:46.005 align:start size:84% position:16%
will actually
follow the student,

00:11:46.005 --> 00:11:49.875 align:start position:0%
whether that is, again, a
homeschool, or a charter school,

00:11:49.875 --> 00:11:52.111
another public school,
or a private school,

00:11:52.111 --> 00:11:54.580
the parent has the
power to decide.

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:55.915
- What would you
say to the people

00:11:55.915 --> 00:11:57.950
on the other side of the
argument who would say,

00:11:57.950 --> 00:12:00.519
well, in essence, this will
just sort of disassemble

00:12:00.519 --> 00:12:02.021
public school teaching,

00:12:02.021 --> 00:12:04.957
and there won't be kind
of any standards anymore,

00:12:04.957 --> 00:12:07.493 align:start size:94% position:6%
and everyone will be
learning different things?

00:12:07.493 --> 00:12:09.895 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, we still
have a state standard

00:12:09.895 --> 00:12:11.397
that you have to meet.

00:12:11.397 --> 00:12:13.833 align:start size:97% position:3%
For a private school, you
get to opt into this program,

00:12:13.833 --> 00:12:15.301
so it's not forced.

00:12:15.301 --> 00:12:17.703
However, I think
the question isn't,

00:12:18.838 --> 00:12:20.406 align:start size:84% position:16%
how does this impact
public schools?

00:12:20.406 --> 00:12:21.640
I think the question would be,

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:24.009 align:start size:84% position:16%
why are people leaving
in the first place?

00:12:24.009 --> 00:12:26.846 align:start position:0%
It's because you're not meeting
the needs of the students.

00:12:26.846 --> 00:12:29.682
And so, I think that Republicans

00:12:29.682 --> 00:12:32.651
have so often been on the
defensive side of this.

00:12:32.651 --> 00:12:34.153
And we've gotta flip the script,

00:12:34.153 --> 00:12:35.454
we've gotta go on offense.

00:12:35.454 --> 00:12:37.490
We have the moral
high ground here.

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:40.126 align:start size:88% position:13%
We are trying to make
sure that every student,

00:12:40.126 --> 00:12:42.928 align:start size:97% position:3%
no matter what their income,
what their background,

00:12:42.928 --> 00:12:44.196
race, color, or creed,

00:12:44.196 --> 00:12:46.665
is getting the best
education possible.

00:12:46.665 --> 00:12:50.469
And we're putting that power
into the hands of parents.

00:12:50.469 --> 00:12:52.938
And we're putting kids
onto a pathway of success

00:12:52.938 --> 00:12:55.808
instead of condemning them
to a lifetime of poverty,

00:12:55.808 --> 00:12:58.544
which is what the other
side wants to see happen.

00:12:58.544 --> 00:13:00.212
- What kind of pushback
did you get on that?

00:13:00.212 --> 00:13:02.548
I mean, I'm guessing
the teacher's union

00:13:02.548 --> 00:13:04.049 align:start size:84% position:16%
and Randi Weingarten,
all of them,

00:13:04.049 --> 00:13:06.218
they probably put in a ton
of money to stop this thing,

00:13:06.218 --> 00:13:07.386
but it happened.

00:13:08.788 --> 00:13:10.990 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You know, I spent two
years on the campaign trail

00:13:10.990 --> 00:13:15.060 align:start size:97% position:3%
here in Arkansas talking
about what we were going to do

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:17.329
in terms of education reform.

00:13:17.329 --> 00:13:20.399
I made no secret that it
was my number one priority.

00:13:20.399 --> 00:13:23.269
And I won with a historic margin

00:13:23.269 --> 00:13:25.771 align:start size:97% position:3%
and for a first time
candidate here in the state,

00:13:25.771 --> 00:13:29.141 align:start size:97% position:3%
and felt like that was because
my message was resonating,

00:13:29.141 --> 00:13:32.611
and that was the people
overwhelmingly coming out

00:13:32.611 --> 00:13:34.814
and saying that this was
what they wanted to see.

00:13:34.814 --> 00:13:36.549 align:start size:84% position:16%
And there was
tremendous support.

00:13:36.549 --> 00:13:39.084
And so, we went out
and we acted on it.

00:13:39.084 --> 00:13:41.020
Could not have done it
without amazing partners

00:13:41.020 --> 00:13:42.521
in our legislature.

00:13:42.521 --> 00:13:44.557
Certainly, there were some
people that pushed back.

00:13:44.557 --> 00:13:46.792
But as my dad likes to say,

00:13:46.792 --> 00:13:48.861 align:start size:88% position:13%
just because some people
eat their soup louder

00:13:48.861 --> 00:13:50.362
doesn't mean it taste better.

00:13:50.362 --> 00:13:52.598 align:start size:84% position:16%
Just because there are
a handful of people

00:13:52.598 --> 00:13:54.233
that are yelling and screaming,

00:13:54.233 --> 00:13:55.734 align:start size:84% position:16%
one, it doesn't
mean they're right,

00:13:55.734 --> 00:13:57.937 align:start size:97% position:3%
and two, it doesn't mean that
they speak for the masses

00:13:57.937 --> 00:14:00.739
'cause that's certainly not
the case here in Arkansas.

00:14:00.739 --> 00:14:04.476 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Do you think the Republicans
have had a branding problem

00:14:04.476 --> 00:14:05.711
over the last couple of years?

00:14:05.711 --> 00:14:07.279 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, if you
look at the midterms,

00:14:07.279 --> 00:14:08.948
everyone said it was
gonna be a red wave.

00:14:08.948 --> 00:14:10.649 align:start size:84% position:16%
It obviously didn't
turn out to be.

00:14:10.649 --> 00:14:13.485 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it seemed that people
were sort of fed up

00:14:13.485 --> 00:14:14.753
with the Democrats,

00:14:14.753 --> 00:14:16.522
but they still didn't
wanna vote Republican

00:14:16.522 --> 00:14:18.557 align:start size:88% position:13%
to some degree, at least
at a national level,

00:14:18.557 --> 00:14:19.892 align:start size:94% position:6%
even though the
Republicans got the House,

00:14:19.892 --> 00:14:22.428
but there clearly wasn't a wave.

00:14:22.428 --> 00:14:25.164
Is there something that
can be done on that,

00:14:25.164 --> 00:14:26.599
if you agree with me.

00:14:26.599 --> 00:14:28.033
- Absolutely.

00:14:28.033 --> 00:14:30.002 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think we, as I was
saying a few minutes ago,

00:14:30.002 --> 00:14:31.804
I think we have
to go on offense.

00:14:31.804 --> 00:14:36.709 align:start size:88% position:13%
So often, Republicans
are so afraid to lean in

00:14:36.709 --> 00:14:38.777
and take the offensive position.

00:14:38.777 --> 00:14:42.114
And we sort of apologize many
times for being conservative.

00:14:42.114 --> 00:14:43.816
And we shouldn't allow the left

00:14:43.816 --> 00:14:45.851 align:start size:81% position:19%
to, one, define
us or demonize us.

00:14:45.851 --> 00:14:48.153 align:start size:84% position:16%
And we have allowed
that for far too long.

00:14:48.153 --> 00:14:50.756 align:start size:88% position:13%
We have to stand up
and start pushing back.

00:14:50.756 --> 00:14:52.424
Whether people like him or not,

00:14:52.424 --> 00:14:55.594 align:start size:97% position:3%
one of the things that Donald
Trump did so successfully

00:14:55.594 --> 00:14:58.564 align:start size:88% position:13%
was he gave Republicans
and conservatives

00:14:58.564 --> 00:15:02.334 align:start size:97% position:3%
a microphone and a platform
to start pushing back and say,

00:15:02.334 --> 00:15:04.970 align:start size:97% position:3%
whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa,
we're not the bad guys here.

00:15:04.970 --> 00:15:06.839
We're not on the side of crazy.

00:15:06.839 --> 00:15:08.474
That's the other side.

00:15:08.474 --> 00:15:11.110
And what they're selling
is frankly just not true.

00:15:11.110 --> 00:15:14.313
He challenged every major
institution in the country,

00:15:14.313 --> 00:15:17.683 align:start size:94% position:6%
whether it was the media,
big tech, higher ed,

00:15:17.683 --> 00:15:19.184
and started pushing back.

00:15:19.184 --> 00:15:22.321
And we're seeing a lot more
Republicans start to do that.

00:15:22.321 --> 00:15:25.591
And I think when we do and
we go on offense, we win.

00:15:27.026 --> 00:15:30.162
- To that point, you actually
gave the Republican response

00:15:30.162 --> 00:15:32.097
to the State of the Union.

00:15:32.097 --> 00:15:34.833 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I thought it was
really excellent.

00:15:34.833 --> 00:15:37.870 align:start position:0%
I thought it was a positive
vision, the type of thing that,

00:15:37.870 --> 00:15:39.305 align:start size:94% position:6%
well, it's really what
you're saying right there.

00:15:39.305 --> 00:15:40.706
There was sort of,

00:15:40.706 --> 00:15:43.442 align:start position:0%
like, we need to use government
effectively when we can.

00:15:43.442 --> 00:15:45.844
But that doesn't seem to me
to be where all Republicans

00:15:45.844 --> 00:15:47.346
are at at the moment.

00:15:47.346 --> 00:15:50.683
You know, if you listen to
Mike Pence or Chris Christie

00:15:50.683 --> 00:15:52.851 align:start size:94% position:6%
on some of the stuff
with DeSantis in Florida,

00:15:52.851 --> 00:15:54.853 align:start size:88% position:13%
that suddenly government
shouldn't do anything

00:15:54.853 --> 00:15:56.755 align:start size:88% position:13%
even when it's to level
the playing field.

00:15:56.755 --> 00:15:59.191 align:start size:94% position:6%
So there's still work
that has to be done there.

00:15:59.191 --> 00:16:00.626
- Absolutely, I think
there's still work.

00:16:00.626 --> 00:16:02.795 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think your opinion
though is the right one,

00:16:02.795 --> 00:16:04.997
that my message is
the good one here.

00:16:04.997 --> 00:16:05.965
No, I'm just kidding.

00:16:05.965 --> 00:16:07.366
(Dave and Sarah laughing)

00:16:07.366 --> 00:16:08.901 align:start size:94% position:6%
I like your take.
- We're going, okay, good.

00:16:08.901 --> 00:16:10.436
- I think we should wrap here

00:16:10.436 --> 00:16:12.204
that you said I did a great job

00:16:12.204 --> 00:16:13.639
and I had the right messaging.

00:16:13.639 --> 00:16:15.140
Only kidding.
- That's it.

00:16:15.140 --> 00:16:18.777
But I do think we have to
draw very clear contrast

00:16:18.777 --> 00:16:20.245
with the other side,

00:16:20.245 --> 00:16:23.015
but we don't have to do it
in a way that is so negative.

00:16:24.183 --> 00:16:27.019
I'm not a glass is half
empty type of person.

00:16:27.019 --> 00:16:29.655
And so, I want to see
the best in people.

00:16:29.655 --> 00:16:34.026 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I still think, regardless
of some of the bad things

00:16:34.026 --> 00:16:35.995
that are happening
around our country,

00:16:35.995 --> 00:16:37.730
that America's still
the greatest country

00:16:37.730 --> 00:16:39.031
that the world has ever known,

00:16:39.031 --> 00:16:40.799
and we shouldn't forget that.

00:16:40.799 --> 00:16:44.003 align:start size:97% position:3%
We have to lean into that
message while at the same time

00:16:44.003 --> 00:16:46.205
drawing a contrast
with the other side.

00:16:46.205 --> 00:16:49.842
That's not negative,
that's simply defining

00:16:49.842 --> 00:16:51.844
who they are versus
what we believe in.

00:16:51.844 --> 00:16:54.913 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think we have to do
that in order to be effective.

00:16:54.913 --> 00:16:57.282
- So one of the things I'm
always trying to do here,

00:16:57.282 --> 00:16:58.550
and it's getting
harder and harder,

00:16:58.550 --> 00:17:01.120
is I really am trying to
sit down with Democrats

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:02.354
when possible.

00:17:02.354 --> 00:17:04.189
We're getting pretty
much no responses.

00:17:04.189 --> 00:17:05.958
These days, we just get ignored.

00:17:05.958 --> 00:17:07.693 align:start size:84% position:16%
But I'm gonna guess,
maybe I'm wrong,

00:17:07.693 --> 00:17:11.897
that a Democrat in Arkansas
is probably not as radical

00:17:11.897 --> 00:17:13.966
as what we're seeing out
of most of the Democrats

00:17:13.966 --> 00:17:16.168 align:start size:84% position:16%
in mainstream
media or out of DC.

00:17:16.168 --> 00:17:17.669
Is that fair to say?

00:17:17.669 --> 00:17:19.538
- Certainly, we've got a
few that are a little bit

00:17:19.538 --> 00:17:21.273
on the very far left end.

00:17:21.273 --> 00:17:24.410 align:start size:84% position:16%
However, we had some
great partnership

00:17:24.410 --> 00:17:26.678
with Democrat legislators here,

00:17:26.678 --> 00:17:28.147
where we were able
to work together

00:17:28.147 --> 00:17:32.451 align:start size:97% position:3%
on a few pieces of legislation
over this first session

00:17:32.451 --> 00:17:34.019
and had great success.

00:17:34.019 --> 00:17:36.255
So certainly, I think
there's a greater sense

00:17:36.255 --> 00:17:39.758 align:start position:0%
of cooperation and
collaboration on the state level

00:17:39.758 --> 00:17:41.660
than you're gonna
find in Washington.

00:17:41.660 --> 00:17:44.930 align:start size:84% position:16%
Washington has
gotten so polarized

00:17:44.930 --> 00:17:46.899
that they've become
completely paralyzed.

00:17:46.899 --> 00:17:50.903 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's hard to expect
or see anything meaningful

00:17:50.903 --> 00:17:51.837
coming from there.

00:17:51.837 --> 00:17:53.105
But I think on the state level,

00:17:53.105 --> 00:17:54.973 align:start size:84% position:16%
you'll find that
there are some moments

00:17:54.973 --> 00:17:57.943 align:start size:97% position:3%
where Democrats and
Republicans can work together.

00:17:59.378 --> 00:18:03.415
- Do you miss anything about
the DC situation and just the,

00:18:03.415 --> 00:18:05.584
there's a certain amount of
energy that comes with it,

00:18:05.584 --> 00:18:07.186
either good, or bad, or both.

00:18:07.186 --> 00:18:08.854
- I definitely miss the people.

00:18:08.854 --> 00:18:11.156
I got to work with, like I said,

00:18:11.156 --> 00:18:14.293 align:start size:94% position:6%
some of the most amazing,
talented individuals

00:18:14.293 --> 00:18:15.727
I've ever encountered.

00:18:15.727 --> 00:18:17.729
And I loved my time there.

00:18:17.729 --> 00:18:20.499
I loved the people that I got
to be in the trenches with.

00:18:20.499 --> 00:18:22.968 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so, I certainly
miss that aspect of it.

00:18:22.968 --> 00:18:25.771 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't miss the nastiness
and the craziness

00:18:25.771 --> 00:18:28.107
of the Washington
press corps though.

00:18:28.107 --> 00:18:29.775
I'm happy to be away from that.

00:18:30.676 --> 00:18:31.877
- Right.

00:18:31.877 --> 00:18:33.112 align:start size:81% position:19%
What else is going
on in Arkansas

00:18:33.112 --> 00:18:35.114 align:start size:84% position:16%
that you're pushing,
trying to lead on

00:18:35.114 --> 00:18:36.348
that we should know about?

00:18:36.348 --> 00:18:37.583
- You know, some of
the biggest things

00:18:37.583 --> 00:18:39.718
that we have really
spent our time on,

00:18:39.718 --> 00:18:43.155 align:start position:0%
we went into this first session
with four big priorities,

00:18:43.155 --> 00:18:45.657 align:start size:84% position:16%
education being
first and foremost,

00:18:45.657 --> 00:18:48.760
and passed what we think
is the most comprehensive,

00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:51.730
historic education package,

00:18:51.730 --> 00:18:53.332
certainly in the
state of Arkansas,

00:18:53.332 --> 00:18:55.400
but probably across the country.

00:18:55.400 --> 00:18:58.103
We spend a lot of time
on workforce development,

00:18:58.103 --> 00:18:59.738
early childhood literacy,

00:18:59.738 --> 00:19:01.974
things that really
lay a foundation

00:19:01.974 --> 00:19:04.977
for an individual's
lifetime of success.

00:19:04.977 --> 00:19:09.114 align:start size:94% position:6%
We've cracked down on
violent repeat offenders.

00:19:09.114 --> 00:19:10.716 align:start size:84% position:16%
That was our second
big priority,

00:19:10.716 --> 00:19:12.951
was a safer, stronger Arkansas.

00:19:12.951 --> 00:19:17.356
We passed legislation that
actually put harsh penalties

00:19:17.356 --> 00:19:20.559 align:start size:97% position:3%
on drug dealers, specifically
those trafficking heroin

00:19:20.559 --> 00:19:24.997 align:start size:97% position:3%
that caused overdoses,
creating truth in sentencing.

00:19:24.997 --> 00:19:26.932 align:start size:84% position:16%
So if somebody gets
sentenced to 20 years,

00:19:26.932 --> 00:19:29.101 align:start size:88% position:13%
we're actually gonna
make you serve 20 years,

00:19:29.101 --> 00:19:31.036
which unfortunately
was not the case here

00:19:31.036 --> 00:19:32.571
and not the case
in a lot of places

00:19:32.571 --> 00:19:34.439
across the state of Arkansas.

00:19:34.439 --> 00:19:37.809
Our third big priority was
cutting the state income tax.

00:19:37.809 --> 00:19:40.579 align:start size:97% position:3%
We've got Texas and
Tennessee on each side of us

00:19:40.579 --> 00:19:42.648
who have no state income tax.

00:19:42.648 --> 00:19:44.883 align:start size:97% position:3%
In order for us to
continue to be competitive,

00:19:44.883 --> 00:19:47.085
we had to work to
start chipping away

00:19:47.085 --> 00:19:48.587
at Arkansas's income tax.

00:19:48.587 --> 00:19:51.223
And we made significant
progress on that front.

00:19:51.223 --> 00:19:54.359 align:start size:97% position:3%
And very proud that we
were able to pass education

00:19:54.359 --> 00:19:56.161
and criminal justice reform,

00:19:56.161 --> 00:20:00.132 align:start position:0%
at the same time, passing
savings back onto the taxpayers.

00:20:00.132 --> 00:20:01.667
The last thing we wanted to do

00:20:01.667 --> 00:20:04.536
was really promote
the natural state.

00:20:04.536 --> 00:20:08.073 align:start position:0%
That's the nickname and the
motto for the state of Arkansas

00:20:08.073 --> 00:20:12.244 align:start size:97% position:3%
because we have an absolutely
beautiful landscape

00:20:12.244 --> 00:20:15.847
and wanna go out and promote
Arkansas's outdoor economy

00:20:15.847 --> 00:20:19.451 align:start size:84% position:16%
and really bring
people into our state

00:20:19.451 --> 00:20:22.821
to enjoy the incredible natural
amenities that we have here.

00:20:22.821 --> 00:20:25.524 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so, those were
the four big key areas

00:20:25.524 --> 00:20:26.959
that we focused on.

00:20:26.959 --> 00:20:29.895 align:start size:94% position:6%
Got something accomplished
in all four of those

00:20:29.895 --> 00:20:31.363
and made what we think

00:20:31.363 --> 00:20:34.233 align:start size:94% position:6%
is just an incredibly
successful first session.

00:20:34.233 --> 00:20:37.302 align:start size:97% position:3%
The other area that
unfortunately we had to spend

00:20:37.302 --> 00:20:39.938
a lot of time on over
the last several weeks,

00:20:39.938 --> 00:20:42.374
Arkansas was hit by
devastating tornadoes

00:20:42.374 --> 00:20:44.076
that came through our state.

00:20:44.076 --> 00:20:46.612
It hit directly here
in Central Arkansas,

00:20:46.612 --> 00:20:49.348
as well as a small town, Wynne,

00:20:49.348 --> 00:20:51.149
over in the eastern
part of our state.

00:20:51.149 --> 00:20:52.918
And we've been in the recovery

00:20:52.918 --> 00:20:55.053
and rebuilding process of that.

00:20:55.053 --> 00:20:56.288
We have a long road to go,

00:20:56.288 --> 00:20:58.523
but very proud of the
way that Arkansans

00:20:58.523 --> 00:21:00.692
have stepped up and really
taken care of each other

00:21:00.692 --> 00:21:02.861
over the last several weeks.

00:21:02.861 --> 00:21:05.097 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Are you getting the help
from FEMA that you need

00:21:05.097 --> 00:21:06.565
or at least that you requested?

00:21:06.565 --> 00:21:07.799
- Oh, we actually have.

00:21:07.799 --> 00:21:10.902
I have to say this is
one of the few areas

00:21:10.902 --> 00:21:13.438 align:start size:97% position:3%
where the federal government
has really stepped up.

00:21:13.438 --> 00:21:17.876
And we're very appreciative
of their willingness to help

00:21:17.876 --> 00:21:20.712
and how quick they
have been to respond

00:21:20.712 --> 00:21:22.981
in providing assistance
that we've requested.

00:21:24.549 --> 00:21:25.851
- Let's back up to the tax one.

00:21:25.851 --> 00:21:27.619
You said cutting
state tax obviously

00:21:27.619 --> 00:21:31.189
because you got no income
tax states on your borders.

00:21:31.189 --> 00:21:33.458
You know, I was in Cali
and now I'm in Florida,

00:21:33.458 --> 00:21:35.727
and I did not move here
for the no tax situation,

00:21:35.727 --> 00:21:38.263
but it has been quite nice.

00:21:38.263 --> 00:21:40.299
I mean, how does that
work when you go in

00:21:40.299 --> 00:21:43.869
and try to cut that tax when
you are a state that has taxes,

00:21:43.869 --> 00:21:45.837
like, nobody that is
working for the state

00:21:45.837 --> 00:21:46.805
is happy with you.

00:21:46.805 --> 00:21:48.707
They wanna keep their jobs,

00:21:48.707 --> 00:21:51.109
but it obviously is gonna
help you build the economy.

00:21:51.109 --> 00:21:53.712 align:start position:0%
- Well, we have to look at ways
that we can make government

00:21:53.712 --> 00:21:56.548
more efficient and
modernize government.

00:21:56.548 --> 00:21:58.116
And there are a lot of places

00:21:58.116 --> 00:22:01.420
where we have duplicate programs

00:22:01.420 --> 00:22:04.022 align:start size:88% position:13%
and we have to eliminate
some of that waste.

00:22:04.022 --> 00:22:07.159 align:start size:94% position:6%
And when we do actually
pass it onto the taxpayer,

00:22:07.159 --> 00:22:10.228 align:start size:88% position:13%
so often, even
conservative Republicans

00:22:10.228 --> 00:22:11.730
will eliminate government waste,

00:22:11.730 --> 00:22:14.266
but then they just add
it into another program.

00:22:14.266 --> 00:22:16.601 align:start size:94% position:6%
We're trying to make sure
that we actually pass

00:22:16.601 --> 00:22:19.538
that savings on in
the form of tax cuts.

00:22:19.538 --> 00:22:21.707 align:start size:88% position:13%
We were successful in
doing that this session.

00:22:21.707 --> 00:22:25.110
And I'm hopeful that we can
continue on that trajectory

00:22:25.110 --> 00:22:27.846
and keep chipping away
at our state's income tax

00:22:27.846 --> 00:22:30.015 align:start size:88% position:13%
until we're able to
completely phase it out.

00:22:30.015 --> 00:22:31.383
It's not gonna happen overnight

00:22:31.383 --> 00:22:33.752 align:start size:97% position:3%
and we wanna be
responsible in the process,

00:22:33.752 --> 00:22:36.154
but it's a goal I think
we have to work towards.

00:22:37.356 --> 00:22:38.824
- Are you telling me
you could cut taxes,

00:22:38.824 --> 00:22:40.759
and your roads won't
automatically collapse,

00:22:40.759 --> 00:22:42.394 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you'll still be
able to pay for school

00:22:42.394 --> 00:22:44.129
and other stuff,
'cause that's wild.

00:22:44.129 --> 00:22:46.598
- Yeah, not only were we
able to pay for schools,

00:22:46.598 --> 00:22:48.967
but we were able to make
the largest investment

00:22:48.967 --> 00:22:52.871
into our schools,
our public schools,

00:22:52.871 --> 00:22:56.708
and education reform in
the history of our state,

00:22:56.708 --> 00:22:58.510
in modern history that is.

00:22:58.510 --> 00:23:00.212
And so, very proud of the fact

00:23:00.212 --> 00:23:02.147
that we were able to get
those things accomplished

00:23:02.147 --> 00:23:04.549
and still cut taxes
here in the state.

00:23:06.118 --> 00:23:08.086
- On the criminal justice
stuff that you mentioned

00:23:08.086 --> 00:23:11.056 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the sentencing,
things of that nature,

00:23:11.056 --> 00:23:12.958
do you get pushback
on those things?

00:23:12.958 --> 00:23:14.693
I would imagine in
a mostly red state,

00:23:14.693 --> 00:23:17.095
people are kind of
on board that, right?

00:23:17.095 --> 00:23:20.399 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Absolutely, we've seen
overwhelming support

00:23:20.399 --> 00:23:22.234
for people coming out.

00:23:22.234 --> 00:23:24.703
Nobody wants to live in a
community that isn't safe.

00:23:24.703 --> 00:23:27.472 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so, locking up
violent repeat offenders

00:23:27.472 --> 00:23:30.776
is rarely going to be something
that is pushed back against,

00:23:30.776 --> 00:23:33.912
unless you're part of
the crazy radical left,

00:23:33.912 --> 00:23:35.814
then you think it's a great idea

00:23:35.814 --> 00:23:37.849 align:start size:88% position:13%
to let those people back
out onto the streets.

00:23:37.849 --> 00:23:41.153 align:start size:97% position:3%
But here in Arkansas, that's
certainly not the case.

00:23:41.153 --> 00:23:42.320
- Right.

00:23:42.320 --> 00:23:43.789
This is a bit of a non-sequitur,

00:23:43.789 --> 00:23:45.924
but does Bill ever
come back down there?

00:23:45.924 --> 00:23:47.926 align:start size:88% position:13%
Is he welcomed in
Arkansas at this point?

00:23:47.926 --> 00:23:51.863
- You know, he's still fairly
well-liked here in Arkansas.

00:23:51.863 --> 00:23:53.732
The Clinton Presidential Library

00:23:53.732 --> 00:23:56.401
is based in our capital
city in Little Rock.

00:23:56.401 --> 00:23:58.870
And he comes back from
time to time for that.

00:23:58.870 --> 00:24:03.775
And, you know, we see him
here very occasionally.

00:24:04.643 --> 00:24:05.877
Hillary, on the other hand,

00:24:05.877 --> 00:24:07.712 align:start size:84% position:16%
not quite so welcome
back in Arkansas.

00:24:07.712 --> 00:24:10.649
I think a lot of people feel
like she abandoned the state.

00:24:10.649 --> 00:24:13.051 align:start size:94% position:6%
And most people I think
are pretty okay with that.

00:24:13.051 --> 00:24:16.188 align:start position:0%
I would say Bill Clinton though
is still fairly well-liked

00:24:16.188 --> 00:24:18.690
and well-remembered here
in the state of Arkansas.

00:24:20.292 --> 00:24:22.394
- So I have to say, as
we wrap up, governor,

00:24:22.394 --> 00:24:24.396 align:start size:84% position:16%
when I was watching
the rebuttal,

00:24:24.396 --> 00:24:26.998 align:start size:97% position:3%
I really was thinking,
man, she should be running.

00:24:26.998 --> 00:24:28.567
I know you've got
a new gig here,

00:24:28.567 --> 00:24:31.670 align:start size:84% position:16%
but we are rolling
into a big 2024 thing.

00:24:31.670 --> 00:24:33.438
The fighting has already begun.

00:24:33.438 --> 00:24:35.173 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's already starting
to get nutty.

00:24:35.173 --> 00:24:36.808
Do you have any of
those aspirations?

00:24:36.808 --> 00:24:38.777
Obviously, your dad ran
for president twice,

00:24:38.777 --> 00:24:40.011
if I'm not mistaken, right?

00:24:40.011 --> 00:24:41.880
And then the first
time, pretty close.

00:24:43.348 --> 00:24:45.083
Does any of that appeal to
you in any way whatsoever?

00:24:45.083 --> 00:24:47.352 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I've done my
time in Washington,

00:24:47.352 --> 00:24:49.421 align:start size:84% position:16%
very happy to be
back home in Arkansas

00:24:49.421 --> 00:24:51.756
where life is a little easier

00:24:51.756 --> 00:24:54.459 align:start size:88% position:13%
and certainly the people
are much friendlier.

00:24:54.459 --> 00:24:55.994
So very happy to be home.

00:24:55.994 --> 00:24:59.064
And I'll leave the
fighting and the 2024

00:24:59.064 --> 00:25:01.500
and all that craziness to
the other folks out there.

00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:04.002 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'm gonna keep my
focus here on our state

00:25:04.002 --> 00:25:05.904 align:start size:84% position:16%
and how we can
really impact change

00:25:05.904 --> 00:25:07.539
and help the people of Arkansas.

00:25:08.907 --> 00:25:10.375 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Final sales pitch
for the people

00:25:10.375 --> 00:25:12.811 align:start size:94% position:6%
that are considering
moving or taking vacation.

00:25:12.811 --> 00:25:15.680
Coming up, why should
they go to Arkansas?

00:25:15.680 --> 00:25:18.216
- You'll find no better place
than the state of Arkansas

00:25:18.216 --> 00:25:20.719
to either live or to visit.

00:25:20.719 --> 00:25:23.021 align:start size:84% position:16%
Certainly, we would
love you to live here.

00:25:23.021 --> 00:25:25.824
I've heard they've got an
amazing governor there.

00:25:25.824 --> 00:25:28.193
If you don't believe me, you
should come try it yourself.

00:25:28.193 --> 00:25:31.229 align:start size:84% position:16%
But we have a really
special place,

00:25:31.229 --> 00:25:34.399 align:start size:81% position:19%
amazing people,
beautiful scenery.

00:25:34.399 --> 00:25:36.902
And we hope that people
will come here and visit

00:25:36.902 --> 00:25:39.905 align:start size:94% position:6%
and decide that they wanna
stay and live here too.

00:25:39.905 --> 00:25:42.240
- Well, governor, I have
only been to Arkansas once,

00:25:42.240 --> 00:25:43.742
believe it or not.

00:25:43.742 --> 00:25:47.245
I did one night there while
on tour about five years ago.

00:25:47.245 --> 00:25:50.782
So I owe a trip and I hope
to get to see you in person.

00:25:50.782 --> 00:25:53.184 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Absolutely, we'd be
happy to host you anytime.

00:25:54.352 --> 00:25:55.754
- All right, Governor
Sarah Huckabee Sanders,

00:25:55.754 --> 00:25:57.022
thanks so much.

00:25:57.022 --> 00:25:58.223
- You bet.

